A|                                       TEASER                                       
O|
N|               AN ABSTRACT IMAGE. A black square against a field of white           
N,G|               and grey.  Quiet, still... kind of mesmerizing. We are               
N,G|               looking at the 4’x7’ Georgia O’Keeffe painting titled “My            
N|               Last Door,” a minimalist representation of the artist’s patio        
N|               door at her home in Abiquiu. This canvas hangs inside...             
O|
S|               INT. ART MUSEUM - DAY                                                
O|
N|               Many of us probably couldn’t identify this work of art, but          
N|               there’s one person who definitely doesn’t have a clue what it        
N|               even is, and that is...                                              
O|
N,G|               ... JESSE, who steps into the shot, his back to us. He looks         
N|               at it, his head tilting a little left, a little right. After         
N|               a moment of silent contemplation...                                  
C|                                   JESSE                                            
U|                         Yo, I thought I was gonna see some,                        
U|                         like, vaginas.                                             
O|
N|               He tells this to a YOUNG WOMAN, who steps into frame next to         
N|               him.  A familiar silhouette against the lighted artwork, and         
N|               we soon reveal this is...                                            
O|
N,I|               JANE.  Yes, Jesse’s beautiful, dear, lost... dead Jane.              
N,M|               Telling us this is another FLASHBACK, sometime after their           
N,M|               planned Santa Fe visit in Episode #209, but before their             
N,M|               downward drug spiral of #211.                                        
C|                                   JANE                                             
U|                         I didn’t say she actually painted                          
U|                         vaginas. I said some of her                                
U|                         paintings looked like vaginas.                             
O|
C|                                   JESSE                                            
P|                             (re: this painting)                                    
U|                         Uh, seriously? Not even close.                             
O|
N|               Jane smiles at his playfulness. He’s just bored and wants to         
N|               get under her skin. But Jane still makes an effort to                
N|               educate him, whispering respectfully in the museum...                
C|                                   JANE                                             
U|                         Georgia O’Keeffe painted all kinds                         
U|                         of stuff. Everyday items.  Her                             
U|                         surroundings. Some of it evoked                            
U|                         an... erotic nature. It’s who she                          
U|                         was.                                                       
O|
N|               Jesse squints at the square black hole in the painting.              
O|
O|                                                                (CONTINUED)         
O|
O|
O|              BREAKING BAD #311         "Abiquiu"         AS BROADCAST     2.       
O|               CONTINUED:                                                           
O|
C|                                   JESSE                                            
U|                         Not like any vagina I ever saw.                            
P|                             (then)                                                 
U|                         This chick have medical issues?                            
C|                                   JANE                                             
U|                         This particular painting is of a                           
U|                         door.                                                      
O|
N|               Jane pads off, leaving Jesse to stare at the abstract portal.        
N|               He frowns, not convinced at all...                                   
O|
C|                                   JESSE                                            
U|                         Any other doors here we can check                          
U|                         out?  Like, real ones, or...?                              
O|
S|               INT. JESSE’S TERCEL - DAY - LATER                                    
O|
N|               The couple climb into Jesse’s Tercel, a lit CIGARETTE in             
N|               Jane’s mouth. Jesse is still a little dumbfounded, holding           
N|               his keys in his hand.                                                
O|
C|                                   JESSE                                            
U|                         Y’know, I-I-I don’t get it. Why                            
U|                         would anyone paint a picture of a                          
U|                         door?  Over and over again, like                           
U|                         dozens of times?                                           
C|                                   JANE                                             
U|                         But it wasn’t the same...                                  
O|
C|                                   JESSE                                            
U|                         Uh, yeah. It was.                                          
O|
C|                                   JANE                                             
U|                         It was the same subject, but it was                        
U|                         different every time. The light                            
U|                         was different, her mood was                                
U|                         different. She saw something new                           
U|                         each time she painted it.                                  
N|               Jesse’s not buying into this artsy-fartsy bullshit.                  
O|
C|                                   JESSE                                            
U|                         And that’s not psycho to you.                              
O|
C|                                   JANE                                             
U|                         Well, then... why should we do                             
U|                         anything more than once? Should I                          
U|                         just smoke this one cigarette?                             
U|                         Maybe we should only have sex once                         
U|                         if it’s the same thing.                                    
O|
O|                                                                (CONTINUED)         
O|
O|
O|              BREAKING BAD #311         "Abiquiu"         AS BROADCAST     3.       
O|               CONTINUED:                                                           
O|
N|               This gets Jesse’s attention... Don’t go down that road.              
O|
C|                                   JESSE                                            
U|                         Whoa.  No...                                               
C|                                   JANE                                             
U|                         Should we just watch one sunset?                           
U|                         Or live just one day?                                      
P|                             (her point)                                            
U|                         Because it’s new every time. Each                          
U|                         time it’s a different experience.                          
O|
N|               Jesse can’t argue with that -- but something is still rubbing        
